            chapters: [
                (
                    "Lecture I — Four distinct questions",
                    """
                    The cardinal virtues provide a basic architecture for moral action. Prudence asks: What is the true good here, and which available means actually serve it? Justice asks: What is owed to God, this person, and the common good? Fortitude asks: What fear, pain, resistance, or weariness must be faced to remain faithful? Temperance asks: Which pleasure, appetite, emotion, or created good must be ordered so it does not take command? These are not four competing personalities. They are dimensions of mature practical love.

                    Prudence is sometimes mistaken for caution or cleverness. In Catholic teaching it is right reason in action. It gathers facts, remembers moral principles, seeks counsel, anticipates consequences, judges, and directs action. Justice is not only courtroom fairness. It includes worship owed to God, truth and fidelity owed in relationships, respect for rights, and concern for structures affecting the common good. Fortitude does more than produce dramatic bravery; it sustains daily fidelity and can restrain reckless aggression. Temperance is not contempt for pleasure; it preserves freedom for genuine enjoyment by keeping desire proportionate.

                    Each virtue has a full course because each requires depth. This orientation gives only the map. The map matters because beginners can otherwise mistake a counterfeit for the real virtue: anxiety for prudence, scorekeeping for justice, aggression for courage, or rigid control for temperance.
                    """
                ),
                (
                    "Lecture II — The hinges must move together",
                    """
                    A good act often needs all four virtues. Imagine discovering a friend's serious dishonesty. Prudence seeks reliable facts, appropriate timing, competent counsel, and a proportionate response. Justice considers those harmed, the friend's dignity, truthful obligations, and any duty to report. Fortitude faces fear of conflict or rejection. Temperance orders anger, curiosity, desire for approval, and the urge to expose the person publicly. Remove any hinge and the door no longer moves well.

                    Courage without prudence becomes rashness; prudence without justice becomes efficient self-interest. Temperance without charity can become sterile control. Justice without fortitude remains an opinion that never protects anyone. The virtues also correct each other's counterfeits. A person may call silence “patience” when justice requires speech, or call an angry outburst “courage” when temperance and prudence require restraint.

                    This unity does not mean every decision is perfectly balanced or obvious. Duties can conflict in appearance, information can be incomplete, and good actions can carry unavoidable cost. The cardinal framework slows moral imagination enough to ask better questions. It also exposes the danger of building an identity around one's favorite virtue while ignoring the one that would correct it.
                    """
                ),
                (
                    "Lecture III — Natural strength, acquired virtue, and grace",
                    """
                    The cardinal virtues can be cultivated through education and repeated acts because human nature is genuinely capable of moral formation. Wisdom 8 names moderation, prudence, justice, and courage as fruits of wisdom useful in human life. This natural intelligibility matters: Christians can recognize genuine courage or justice outside visible Church boundaries and cooperate in common goods.

                    Yet sin wounds judgment and desire, and natural excellence is not the whole of Christian holiness. CCC 1810–1811 teaches that human virtues are acquired by practice but are purified and elevated by grace. Grace heals their distortions and orders them toward friendship with God. A courageous person may need grace not simply to endure more, but to forgive. A disciplined person may need freedom from pride. A shrewd planner may need conversion from self-protection to service.

                    Titus 2 locates self-control, uprightness, and godliness inside saving grace and hope. Micah joins justice, mercy, and humble walking with God. These passages keep the cardinal virtues from becoming a self-contained program of classical respectability. Christian prudence, justice, fortitude, and temperance are human excellences taken up into discipleship and animated by charity.
                    """
