                    """
                    The cardinal questions must be applied to the whole act, not only its desired result. A noble intention cannot make an unjust means good. A lawful action can still become selfish through a corrupt intention. Circumstances can increase or lessen responsibility and can make an otherwise good choice imprudent, though they cannot turn an intrinsically evil act into a good one.

                    Prudence identifies the real act and available means rather than accepting a flattering description. Justice tests those means against what persons are due. Fortitude prevents fear of cost from rewriting the moral facts. Temperance restrains the urge to seize a quick result at any price. Together they protect moral action from the slogan “the end justifies the means.”

                    This fuller view also improves examination. Instead of asking only whether the outcome felt successful, ask: What did I choose? Why did I choose it? What relevant circumstances did I consider or ignore? Whose rights and goods were involved? Which virtue was present, which was absent, and what repair or new judgment is now required?
