                    Sanctifying grace is not merely information or external approval. Catholic teaching speaks of a real participation in divine life, a stable supernatural gift that heals and elevates the soul. Actual graces are particular helps for conversion and sanctification. This language can seem technical, but its purpose is pastoral: Christianity announces not simply better behavior but a new life received from God.

                    Because grace is gift, no learner can use completed lessons, streaks, or visible habits to conclude that God owes them favor. CCC 2005 warns that grace belongs to the supernatural order and escapes direct experience; we cannot infer our justification from feelings or works. An app may record what was read or practiced. It cannot display a holiness score or certify a state of grace.
