                    God's initiative does not erase human freedom. Grace heals and calls forth a real response. CCC 2001 teaches that the preparation of the person for grace is already a work of grace, and that God's action elicits human cooperation. We pray, repent, confess, repair, practice, seek counsel, resist temptation, and receive the sacraments because God is at work—not because human action has become unreal.

                    Two errors distort this relationship. Self-salvation turns Christian life into optimization: if I design the perfect routine, I can secure holiness and superiority. Passivity uses grace as an excuse: if God must act first, my avoidance is irrelevant. Catholic cooperation refuses both. Apart from Christ we can do nothing; within grace, our consent and action truly belong to us and truly matter.

                    This has practical consequences. Someone praying for patience may need to stop rehearsing grievances, sleep adequately, apologize, delay speech, and seek help. Someone building excellent habits may need to kneel, receive mercy, and admit that discipline cannot conquer sin or death. Prayer and practice are partners only when practice remains dependence and prayer becomes available for obedience.
