                    This ecclesial setting protects formation from self-invention. Alone, a person can select only teachings that confirm existing preferences, define growth by personal comfort, and avoid anyone with authority to correct. Community is not automatically healthy or right, and grave failures within the Church must never be minimized. Yet the abuse of authority does not make solitary self-authentication a sufficient alternative. Authentic ecclesial life includes truth, accountability, protection of the vulnerable, and the possibility of appeal and wise counsel.

                    Lumen Gentium teaches a universal call to holiness across states of life. Holiness is not reserved for clergy or religious, nor is it an identical lifestyle imposed on everyone. The parent, student, worker, spouse, single person, priest, and religious live distinct responsibilities. Virtue gives each vocation reliable forms of love.
