                    Luke 24 gives a pattern: Christ accompanies confused disciples, listens to their account, interprets Scripture, accepts hospitality, is recognized in the breaking of bread, and sends them back to the community. Formation includes explanation, sacramental recognition, changed desire, and mission. It is not information transfer alone.

                    John 13 places service at the center. Jesus' washing of feet is not generic niceness; it flows from his identity, love, and coming self-gift. The disciples must receive before they imitate. Liturgical communion that never reaches embodied service is incomplete, while service detached from worship can forget the source and destiny of love. The ordinary school holds both together.
