            chapters: [
                (
                    "Lecture I — Virtue has a face",
                    """
                    Christian virtue education does not end with possession of admirable traits. Jesus Christ is the living center and measure of the Christian life. He is prudent without manipulation, just without coldness, courageous without cruelty, temperate without contempt for creation, humble without denying his mission, and charitable to the end. These descriptions are not labels attached from outside. The virtues find unity in the way he knows, loves, obeys, suffers, serves, and gives himself.

                    Christians do not merely extract principles from Jesus as from an ancient casebook. They abide in him, receive his grace, hear his word, participate in his sacramental life, imitate his deeds, and are conformed to him by the Spirit. Matthew 11 presents discipleship as taking his yoke and learning from his meek and humble heart. The teacher gives not only information but rest and a shared manner of carrying life's burden.

                    This prevents virtue from becoming self-display. If Christ is the center, the question changes from “How impressive am I becoming?” to “How can I receive and embody his love in this responsibility?” Hidden patience with a dependent relative may matter more than a public act of daring. An apology may show more fortitude than a dramatic confrontation. Christlike virtue is recognized by truth and self-gift, not branding.
                    """
                ),
                (
                    "Lecture II — Growth lasts a lifetime",
                    """
                    Finishing a course means lessons were studied. It does not mean the learner has mastered a virtue, attained holiness, or earned a spiritual rank. Virtues interact, circumstances change, deeper motives emerge, wounds need healing, and new responsibilities expose untrained places. Growth includes consolation and dryness, success and failure, action and patient waiting.

                    Philippians 3 holds together genuine participation in Christ and the admission that the goal has not been fully attained. Paul presses forward because he has been taken hold of by Christ. Second Peter describes qualities that should increase, not boxes checked once. Perseverance is therefore neither anxious perfectionism nor comfortable stagnation. It is repeated return to grace and the next faithful act.

                    Failure must be handled within this lifelong horizon. Sin calls for repentance, confession when appropriate, repair, and renewed practice. It does not justify despair or concealment. Success calls for gratitude and vigilance rather than superiority. The mature learner becomes more teachable, not less. Increased knowledge should enlarge awareness of dependence and the complexity of loving real persons.
                    """
                ),
                (
                    "Lecture III — Choosing the next course truthfully",
                    """
                    After this foundation, the learner may enter a cardinal virtue course or one of the seven courses opposing the capital sins. The choice should not be driven by whichever title produces the most shame or whichever strength already wins admiration. Begin with vocation and present duty. Where is love currently being asked of you? Which repeated difficulty makes that duty harder? Which virtue would illuminate and support the next faithful response?

                    Counsel can expose blind spots. A spouse, trusted friend, priest, spiritual director, or therapist may see patterns that private reflection misses. Practical readiness matters too. Someone in crisis may first need safety, rest, treatment, or stabilization rather than an ambitious formation plan. A person with scrupulosity may need to avoid obsessive tracking. Formation serves the person; the person does not exist to feed the system.

                    Write plans provisionally. A good plan names a desired good, one present responsibility, one virtue to study, several supports, a likely counterfeit, and a small repeated practice. It also includes review: What fruit is appearing? Who is affected? What needs repair? Has the practice become vanity or avoidance? The goal is a life continually teachable before Christ, not permanent dependence on an app.
                    """
                ),
                (
                    "Lecture IV — Review by fruit, repair, and renewed fidelity",
                    """
                    A formation plan needs periodic review because even a good practice can drift. Ask what has become more truthful and loving, which people bear the effects, and whether the practice supports actual duties. Look for hidden costs: irritability, neglect, superiority, compulsive tracking, or avoidance disguised as study. Invite someone trustworthy to challenge the story you tell about your own progress.

                    Review must include repair. If new insight reveals harm, the next step may be apology, restitution, disclosure, changed boundaries, or qualified help rather than another lesson. Knowledge that never reaches repair can protect the very vice it names. Conversely, not every slow result means the plan failed. Some duties require quiet perseverance without dramatic feedback.

                    End review by choosing renewed fidelity, not by issuing a verdict on your worth. Keep what serves love, revise what does not, and return to prayer and sacrament. The app should gradually become a lighter support as wisdom, community, and stable practice grow. Its success is measured partly by helping the learner live beyond the screen.
                    """
                ),
